# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings are load-bearing in this project — see docs/PLAN.md §8.1.
#
# A fixture.sh checked out with CRLF fails in bash on the stray \r, with an
# error that points nowhere near the real cause. And because fixture commit
# SHAs must match byte-for-byte across Windows and Linux CI (§6.1), blob
# content has to be identical on both — which means LF, everywhere, always.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Normalize all text in the repository; check out LF on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two that would actually break. Explicit for the benefit of the next
# person to read this file.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.cmds  text eol=lf

# Genuinely need CRLF to run on Windows.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Binary — never normalize, never diff.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.db    binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# Keep vendored and generated files out of diffs and language statistics.
#
# `-text` is the load-bearing half, and it is the one exception to the LF rule
# above. These four files are pinned by SHA-256 in
# src/practice/interface/web/vendor.py, so what git checks out has to be the
# bytes that were downloaded and nothing adjacent to them. `text=auto` would
# normalize them on the way in, which is harmless on its own and not harmless
# the moment anything hashes the result.
src/practice/interface/web/static/vendor/** -text linguist-vendored
uv.lock -diff linguist-generated

# Drawn by docs/demo/render.py from docs/demo/session.txt. Still LF text — it
# is reviewable and the generator is the thing to review — but neither a diff
# nor a language this project is written in.
docs/demo.svg -diff linguist-generated
